Quick Information: 
#include <unordered_map>
'std::unordered_map' is a container in C++ that stores ley-value pairs. It is optimized for fast lookup by key. 
    -> std::unordered_map is a hash table that lets you associate a key with a value.
       std::unordered_map<std::string, int> ages;
        ages["Alice"] = 24;
        ages["Bob"] = 31;
        std::cout << ages["Alice"];    output-> "24" 
  
      In the above example, the unordered_map uses a type of "string" as the key and a type "int" as a value. 

C++ Container Ecosystem
  std::unordered_map is an associative container. 
    -> Keys are unsorted
    -> Accessed by index
    -> Usually implemented using a hash table. 

Basic Syntax
  std::unordered_map<keyTpe, ValueType> VariableName; -> std::unordered_map<std::string, int> ages;
  Key-> string
  Value-> Int
  VariableName-> ages

Complexity
Operation        Average        Worst Case-> (Hashing Collisions)
Intert           0(1)           0(n)
Find             0(1)           0(n)
Erase by Key     0(1)           0(n)
Access[]         0(1)           0(n)

Implementation and Use-Cases
    Unordered maps tend to use hashing systems. 
    An unordered map can only have one value per key, unless you use something like a vector for a value. You could also use a std::unordered_multimap. 
    For example:
        std::unordered_map<std::string, int> example; 
        example[sample1] = 1;
        example[sample1] = 7;            
    sample1 will only equal 7.
        std::unordered_map<std::string, std::vector<int>> example; 
        example[sample1].push_back(1);
        example[sample1].push_back(8);       
    sample1 will equal (1, 8)
    Sequence containers need to be iterated,* but the compiler already knows where the element should be in memory from the key-class in an unordered_map.
        (i.e.,In an unordered_map, you have a 'map' to the key. You don't have to find the key from a sequence of keys).
    Each element is essentially a std::pair<const Key, Value>
        Which has .first (key), .second (value)
  
Functions
mapName.empty() -> O(1)
    Returns a 'bool' type
    returns true if there are no elements

mapName.size() -> O(1)
    returns a "integer" type
    returns the number of the elements present

mapName[key] = value -> O(1)
    Looks up the key.
    If the key does not exist, the operator[] creates the key and sets its value to zero at first. 
    The operator[] could accientally create a key. 

mapName.find(key) -> O(1)
    .find(key) does not insert anything. 
    Returns an iterator to that pair, or the end iterator

mapName.at() -> O(1)
    Does not insert a missing key
    If the key does not exist, it throws std::out_of_range

C++ 20+ 
mapName.contains(key) -> O(1)
    returns bool
    returns true if the key is found
    Does not insert

mapName.insert(key, value) or mapName.insert(std::make_pair(key, value)); -> O(1)
    Can insert multiples ({ {k1,v1}, {k2, v2}, ...});

mapName.emplace(key, element) -> Amortized Constant
    Returns a pair of an iterator and a bool
    If the element already exists, it returns and terator point to the inserted element. 
    If the element does not exist, it reutns an iterator to the newly added container. 
    Can optimize performance
    emplace() can avoid unnecessary temporary objects.
    Inserts an element. 

C++ 17
mapName.try_emplace("Key", constructor_argument); -> O(1)
    The mapped object is only constructed if the key doesn't already exist. 
    Avoids unnecessary object construction. 
    Returns whether the insertion was successful. 

mapName.insert_or_assign() -> O(n)
    Returns std::pair<iterator, bool>
    The iterator points to the element that was inserted/assigned
    Bool returns true if the insertion took place
    Bool returns false if an assignment took place
    Best for expenseive-to-construct types (vector, objects, etcs)
    Inserts stuff (duh :P)

Examples
1. Creating an Empty Unordered Maps
std::unordered_map<std::string, int> ages;

2. Inerting Elements With operator[]
ages["key"] = value; 

3. Using an Iterator
auto it = ages.find(key);
    it->first outputs the key
    it->second outputs the value

4. Range-Based Loops
For (const auto& pair : ages) {
    pair.first;
    pair.second; }
Or
for(const auto& [key, value] : ages){
std::cout << key ":" << value; }
//for every key/value pair... keyName = key; valueName = value; 

5. Using .at()
try{ std::count << ages.at("Nobody); }
catch(const std::out_of_range& e){ std::cout << "Key not found"; } 

6. Emplacing Complicated Types
data.emplace( "score", std::vector<int>{10, 20, 30} );

7. Using .try_emplace
std::unordered_map<std::string, MyObject> objects;
objects.try_emplace("Alice", constructor_argument1);
Or
map.try_emplace("a", 123); 

8. Using .insert_or_assign()
map.insert_or_assign(Key, Value);
